BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: I take the bowl from the hearth and eat. Does anyone speak?
  DIRECTOR: The bowl is warm in Bresk's hands and tastes of salt, root, and someone else's rationing. Four hundred stopped organs hang overhead like a ceiling that forgot to be a ceiling, and one still hand holds something half-folded, waiting to finish.

The one who put the bowl out is broad, grey at the fold, worn down to patience, and does not lower their voice because they don't need to. "Yes," they say, loud enough for all four hundred. "And whose wage are you on?" The soup steam curls up into absolutely still air, and every body on the Row is waiting for an answer they can hear.

Bresk, you said your wage out loud at the gate and the Row heard it — what do you say to the steward, in front of the silent machines?
