ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: What does the quiet do to my work? Can I mend anything here?
  DIRECTOR: Ilm holds up a hand toward the nearest silent organ, fingers testing the flow of chemistry from its surface — and feels it. The brine here is flat. Nothing is being printed, nothing is being folded, nothing is being mended. The tools that would run a Synthesis in a living district simply will not catch here; the chemistry has no purchase. The soup steam doesn't curl because it has nowhere to go. This is the Silence: four hundred organs stopped mid-cycle, and the whole Row is dry of process. Whatever Ilm can carry, she cannot run here.

The steward has not looked away from Bresk, and the four hundred are still waiting for an answer that is not just to them.

Bresk, the Row heard you say the cheapest wage at the gate — what do you say to the steward now, in front of all of them?
