  DIRECTOR: The arch closes behind you and the Print Floor hits you warm — brine thick as syrup, the smell of fresh print, and under your feet a steady working rhythm that never once faltered. The Line runs at Mid, unattended, on a schedule nobody signed. On the far table its output sits folded and legible: nine Beats of the same thing, in pieces, nearly finished — and a fresh print laid down while you watched, warm, one fold deeper than the rest, so clear you can read the whole shape of it at a glance. That shape is the shape from the patent.

At Mid, past the output shelf, an attendant with a towel and an appointment book asks, soothing and relentless, whether any of you have an appointment. Far across the room, one very large body stands absolutely still among the prints.

Brost — your read on that deeper fold tells you something specific about the document you were paid to bring back. What do you do with it?

ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: Why did the Line jam? What do the three observations say?
  DIRECTOR: Ilm leans into organ 118 and reads the jam: it is not broken, it is stalled — the Line has been printing a fold the Belt's grammar forbids, an inherited body shape, and catches on it every pass, one fold deeper. The half-fold sits in the press, physical, waiting for hands. A contradictory specification fed in would freeze the cycle clean.

Ilm — do you put your hands on the press now?
