  DIRECTOR: The Print Floor does not stop for you. A steady working rhythm runs under the floor, and the smell of fresh print is everywhere — warm, clean, industrial. The Line hums at Near, printing on its own schedule with nobody at the wheel. At Mid, the output shelf holds nine Beats of the same folded thing, stacked in pieces, all one shape, all nearly finished: whatever is waiting in the alley behind organ 41, this machine has been printing it here, blind, for nine Beats. A parlour attendant drifts up with a towel, soothing and relentless: "Do you have an appointment, or shall I fit you in?" And at the far end, one very large person stands perfectly still, not working, not leaving.

Ilm, you quoted Shift Nine's output price out loud in front of an Auditor — and that folded shape on the shelf is the output. Do you go to the Line to read what it's making, or to the still figure first?

ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: Why did the Line jam? What do the three observations say?
    [reveal_secret] {"secret":"body-trait","how":"Ilm read the Line's input sequence directly and identified it as a Pristine Sequence — a living body's own inherited trait, not an industrial fold."}
  DIRECTOR: Ilm puts her claws on organ 118 and the Line's own rhythm answers her. The print head is reaching for a seam that isn't there — the fold it was fed has no third attachment point, so it closes and closes and never latches. What it was fed is not an industrial fold at all: it is a Pristine Sequence, the shape a living body carries and hands down, which the Belt's grammar does not permit anyone to license. And the fold sitting half-done in the organ is that same shape, stalled mid-close — physical proof the specification demands something no machine here can print. The shelf read came out muddied at exactly one point, which is why she needed the jam to say it.

Near, on the moving Line, that half-finished fold is loose and traveling toward the crush. Vash of the Long Reach — can you take it off the Line before the next stroke flattens it?

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: We speak to the Overseer. What does it want from us?
