BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: Yes. Nothing more to cut. We go now.
  DIRECTOR: The nine-badged one does not step aside. It plants across the corridor mouth, clipboard braced, and writes the charge a third time: removing licensed material from organ one hundred and eighteen — the wrong address, and forty shells heard the warranty say so. Behind it the constructs hold their diagram squares and the tank ticks its hourly rate; none of them will strike first, but the corridor is shut.

And the bench moves without you. "Ticket nine," the clerk calls, and a gallery organism rises to its shell, and Eleven-Brief turns the drafted decision face-up and uncaps a stylus. If it signs before you are back at the bench, the Foundry keeps the patent and the hearing is lost by walking out.

Ilm of the Open Hand — the decision is opening on the bench right now. Do you go back and finish the hearing before that stylus lands, or let it sign and shove for the alley through the Relief?

VASH OF THE LONG REACH: I talk to the print. It asks if it is finished. What do I tell it?
  DIRECTOR: The construct turns one brass joint toward Vash without leaving its square. "The assignment is not closed," it says mildly. "When the charge is filed, the specification ends." The nine-badged one's stylus pauses — it is waiting for the charge to be filed too, and filing needs the address correct.

At the bench, Eleven-Brief's stylus is descending toward the face-up decision.

Ilm — the construct stopped on its own specification. Can you make the warranty a document their joints will obey?
