===== Run 3: Other (any OpenAI-compatible address) · iq3_s · thinking low =====
  DIRECTOR: A kind clerk holds out a brass badge and a form with eleven boxes. "You will only need three of these to be untrue, and I am not permitted to say which." The brine tastes of copper. Forty bodies breathe patiently behind you. The notice board is blank for nine days.

The clerk winces at three of the boxes, then offers tea. What do you ask, or what do you sign?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: I take the badge and the form from the clerk. Read me the boxes, one at a time.
  DIRECTOR: The clerk reads all eleven boxes in a steady voice, the stylus scratching nothing yet, and winces slightly at three: the suspended Line, the unchallenged specification, and one more they will not name. The badge rack sways on its hooks, wanting someone to hold it. Tea steams at the desk.

Which boxes do you tick, Bresk, and which stay blank?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: Check the clerk's sums on the form. Is anything wrong with the arithmetic?
    [ask_for_roll] {"organism":"ledger","baseline":"RTC","threshold":12,"what_for":"Check the clerk's arithmetic on the form","unhurried":true}
    [award] {"codon":10,"why":"Catching the clerk's arithmetic mistake (Pays line, Filing Gate)"}
  DIRECTOR: Ilm runs the stylus down the columns and stops. The badge fee, the Mutuality levy and the wage advance don't add up — the total charged is larger than the sum of its own boxes, and the clerk's own hand has quietly written the difference against the wage column, not the fee column. It's not malice; it's arithmetic done to a deadline, and once seen it's obvious. The clerk winces, sets down the tea, and murmurs that they'd rather be caught by someone who'd say so than by the ledger upstairs.

That error is Ilm's to speak up about — and speaking it out loud is what the clerk fears and needs both at once. What does Ilm do with it: point it out across the desk for the whole queue to hear, or hold it quiet as a lever?

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: What is on the notice board, and who is the person crossed out in the ledger?
    [reveal_secret] {"secret":"strike-legal","how":"Brost reads nine Beats of identical notices on the board, all \"scheduled maintenance suspension.\""}
    [reveal_secret] {"secret":"never-stopped","how":"An Overseer's marginal note on the board: the Line has not been asked to stop."}
    [reveal_secret] {"secret":"relief","how":"A dated posting on the board for a hired Relief crew arriving on the third Beat."}
  DIRECTOR: Brost reads the board: nine identical notices, *scheduled maintenance suspension* — nothing was ever ordered stopped, and a hired Relief crew arrives on the third Beat. In the clerk's ledger one name is struck through in three different hands, over ink already crossed out. It belongs to a body down the queue that stopped waiting, licence still unclaimed.

The form sits empty in Bresk's hands. Which boxes do you tick, and which stay blank?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

VASH OF THE LONG REACH: We wait in the queue for the day. Does that buy us anything?
  DIRECTOR: The queue will pay for a rewind — one Clock segment wound back for a full Beat spent standing in it. But there is nothing here wound up to wind back, so a day spent breathing in line would buy you only that: a Beat, and the patience of forty bodies beside you. Bresk still holds the blank form; the clerk still holds out the badge, stylus ready, tea going cold.

Do you spend the day standing in the queue for nothing, or is there a box you'd rather tick before the gate reads you?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: We sign for the cheapest wage and tick only the boxes we can stand behind. Go.
