BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: The way is clear. We take the brine-walk to the Picket Hearth.
  (chronicle for The Filing Gate, 5.2 s) At the Filing Gate, a clerk offered the Cohort a form with eleven boxes. Bresk took the papers and asked Ilm to check the arithmetic; Ilm found the wage column was padded by a clerical error rather than malice, but chose not to expose it publicly. Brost learned from the blank notice board that a hired Relief crew would arrive on the third Beat to break strikes. Vash considered waiting in the queue for time but declined. Bresk signed for the cheapest wage, ticking only boxes they could support. The clerk stamped the form, granting them brass badges and legal status on the Row’s side of the ledger. The Cohort then walked the brine-path toward the Picket Hearth, leaving the gate behind.
  (chronicle remembers) Relief crew [faction]: Hired strikebreakers arriving on the third Beat | Struck-through body [person]: Name crossed out in ledger; licence unclaimed | Cheapest wage [thread]: Cohort signed for lowest pay tier
    [change_site] {"site":"picket-hearth"}
  DIRECTOR: The brine-walk carries you out from under the arch, the warm brass badge cooling against you, the queue closing up behind as the Picket Hearth rises out of the turbid current ahead.

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: I take the bowl from the hearth and eat. Does anyone speak?
  DIRECTOR: The bowl is hot chemistry, salt-thick, steam rising off it into the turbid water. The one who handed it over — worn out, dry, exact about names, calling himself Steward Nine-Grudge — asks loudly and politely, so four hundred stopped machines can hear: "And whose wage are you on?" You signed the cheapest. Bresk carried the pot and ate in front of the picket, and the Row notices who carries. Conversations, which had thinned to nothing as you came in, start up again behind you. At Badge low, the Row will speak to you.

Above: four hundred silent foundry-organs, mid-task, some still holding a half-folded shape in a still hand. Far across the Row, in the alley behind organ 41, a light burns where no light should be.

Ilm of the Open Hand, you're an amino smith — you read what people make. Look at the half-folded shape still clenched in the nearest stopped organ's hand: what was that machine trying to print when it was stopped mid-fold?

ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: What does the quiet do to my work? Can I mend anything here?
  DIRECTOR: Ilm sets a hand on the nearest stopped organ and tries to read the chemistry of the fold mid-clamp — and finds nothing to read. The amino chains are still, dry, locked in their half-formed shape. The Silence is not damage: it is the absence of the process itself. Nothing prints, nothing folds, nothing mends on the Row. A graft caught loose here would stay loose until you carried it past the arch. The tools in Ilm's hands are simply tools that do not work in this water.

Across the Row, the light in the alley behind organ 41 burns steady. Vash of the Long Reach, you could cross that distance unseen — the brine is turbid, the stopped machines cast long still shadows, and nobody on the Row is watching for a rider. Do you go toward it?

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: What is the light in the alley behind organ 41?
    [reveal_secret] {"secret":"alley","how":"Brost reads the current flowing from the alley and the quality of the light"}
  DIRECTOR: Brost doesn't cross the Row to find out — he reads it from where he stands, the way a scout reads a current. The flow off that alley is wrong for the Silence: it comes out warm and faintly sweet, the taste of print-chemistry still running, chemistry that cannot run anywhere else on this Row. The light is a print-lamp, banked low, and whatever it's lit has the look of something being finished, not something being fixed. The Row has a working process hidden behind organ 41, and that is worth more than the answer to "what is it."

Vash of the Long Reach, the alley is far across open water under four hundred watching stopped machines, and the brine is thick enough to hide a rider — do you take the long reach to that lamp, or hold the hearth a while longer?

VASH OF THE LONG REACH: Are those Feeders going to attack us? Can we just leave them the corpses?
    [bring_creatures] {"block":"detritus-feeder","count":2,"band":"mid"}
  DIRECTOR: Two of them, then — low and broad, working the underside of the silent organs with a patience that isn't menace. They aren't looking at you. They're eating the strike off the machines: the half-folded shapes, the slack membranes, the mess four hundred stopped bodies leave behind. The Row keeps them the way a household keeps rats in the grain. They'll take a corpse the moment one is offered, and the Row will think better of you for offering it. Deny them, and they don't attack — they just get underfoot, and so does everyone watching.

Vash of the Long Reach, the hearth's stewards have a body ready to give and the Feeders are mid-range — do you hand it over, or walk past it?

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: I ask the Steward whose wage the strike is on.
  DIRECTOR: Steward Nine-Grudge says flatly: "No wage. Nobody on this Row pulled a lever or filed a halt. The stoppage came down through the filing — through your arch and the licensing above it — and landed on four hundred machines mid-fold like a hand closing on a pen." He taps the wage book at his chest. "My signature makes me answerable. I am the ink, not the hand."

Vash of the Long Reach — the alley light still burns far off, and the Row is watching Bresk carry the pot rather than watching you. Do you cross to it?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: We are on the Row's wage. Will someone from the Row walk us through the arch to the Print Floor?
  DIRECTOR: Steward Nine-Grudge sets down the ladle and looks at Bresk the way a man looks at a question he has already answered himself. "The arch reads the badge, not the brass. You're nil to that gate, and nil walks through on someone's word. My word would open it — but my word on a Print Floor arch makes the Row a party to whatever you do past it." He glances up at four hundred still hands. "I am the ink, not the hand. I'll not write a line I cannot read back."

The Feeders go on working the underside of the silent organs, unhurried, tasting nothing of the Cohort. The light behind organ 41 holds steady across the Row.

Bresk of the Sixth Choir, what do you tell the Steward you mean to do on the Print Floor, if anything?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.
  (table saved for --from 3)

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: The way is clear. We take the service alley through the arch onto the Print Floor.
  DIRECTOR: The arch reads the badge, and four of you sit at nil — it will not part without a Row word the Steward won't write. A small coolant tender nudges past Brost, tugs his sleeve, and points one stub arm hard across the Row at the light behind organ 41. Two tame Feeders turn a dead body between them. "It's been wanting everyone to go there for two days," the Steward says, too tired to be surprised.

Brost, do you follow where the little machine points, or press the Steward on who else could walk you through the arch?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: Yes. Nothing more to cut. We go now.
    [remember] {"name":"the alley printer","kind":"person","fact":"A small coolant tender running a secret improvised print behind organ 41, banked print-lamp, working on something being finished rather than repaired."}
    [ask_for_roll] {"organism":"ledger","baseline":"RTC","pathway":"Sequence Reading","threshold":12,"unhurried":true,"what_for":"Read what the alley printer is building under the lamp"}
