  DIRECTOR: Ilm leans under the banked lamp and reads the fold in plain chemistry. What the little tender is printing is the shape four hundred stopped hands still hold half-clenched — but here it completes. It is no patented print at all. It is an inherited fold, a living body's own nature. One lamp, one running line, and the Silence ends in this compartment. The tender waits to see what you do with the sight.

Ilm — say it out loud, and to whom: the tender, the Cohort, or the Steward's ears across the Row?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: What has the Line made? Read me what is on the output shelf.
  DIRECTOR: The shelf above the lamp is stacked neat: row on row of the fold, finished whole — the shape four hundred stopped hands still hold half-clenched, but closed and living. None carries a seal. It is a body's own nature, made by hand. The tender watches the Cohort the way a nervous clerk watches a witness.

Vash of the Long Reach, what do you tell the tender before the Row notices that lamp?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: Why did the Line jam? What do the three observations say?
  DIRECTOR: Two of the three observations are already in front of you: the fold whole on the shelf, and the print chemistry running alive under this lamp. The third is the Line's own feed state — what its machines were drawing on when they froze — and that lives past the arch, in the Silence's compartment. Nothing jammed; the stoppage came down through the filing, not a lever.

Vash, the lamp is yours to leave unseen. Do you cross back to the hearth with what you've seen, or push for organ 118's cage first?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: We speak to the Overseer. What does it want from us?
  DIRECTOR: There's no Overseer on the Row, and the Steward says so before the question is fairly out of the water. "The one you're hunting wears a seal, not an apron, and his floor is past that arch — where your stoppage came from, not where it landed." He gestures at the four hundred still hands without looking up. "On this side the only signature is mine, and I've already told you what mine is worth." The soup steam drifts sideways in the dead current, tasting of salt and something scorched.

Bresk of the Sixth Choir — if it's the sealed authority you want, the only door to it is that arch, and it wants a badge or a Row word the Steward won't give; so do you press the Steward for the word, or find another way onto licensed ground?

VASH OF THE LONG REACH: I ask the Parlour attendant what it can fold for us, and read the warranty aloud.
  DIRECTOR: No parlour on the Row, and the Steward says so without looking up from his ladle. "You want warranty talk, you want a seal. This is soup." But he sets the ladle down and turns to face all four of them for the first time. "The relief crew comes Beat three. Four hundred bodies still holding their folds, and I am the ink on their halt." He taps the wage book. "If you carry the grievance — name it, out loud, where the Row can hear — I'll walk you through that arch myself."

Bresk of the Sixth Choir — the Steward is offering the arch in exchange for a public grievance. Do you name it?

ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: We feed the Line a Row grievance and see what it spends the day on.
  DIRECTOR: Ilm's words go out flat and plain, and a murmur moves hand to hand along four hundred stopped machines. The Steward weighs the sentence against the wage book, then gives one short nod. "That I can read back." He hangs the ladle and pulls his apron straight. "Then I walk you. Stay in my sightline."

Bresk — the Steward is moving toward the arch. Do you fall in and step onto licensed ground now?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.
  (table saved for --from 4)

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: The way is clear. We go up the pump-lift to the Licence Court.
  DIRECTOR: The Steward is already halfway down the service alley, apron straight, not looking back. There is no pump-lift on the Row and no court above it; the only licensed ground within walking distance is past that arch, on the Print Floor, and the only way through it is the Steward's sightline and his word. He stops at the Warden arch and turns, one stub hand resting on the gate-frame. The two tame Feeders have found a collapsed organ between them and are pulling it apart with small patient sounds, unhurried, ignoring everyone.

Bresk — the Steward is waiting under the arch. Do you walk into his sightline, or does someone stay behind?

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: Yes. Nothing more to cut. We go now.
  (chronicle for The Picket Hearth, 6.4 s) Bresk ate at the Picket Hearth, drawing attention to their nil badge status. Ilm confirmed the Silence froze printing processes, but Brost spotted a hidden light behind organ 41. The cohort discovered a coolant tender completing an inherited biological fold there, proving the halted patented shape was actually natural biology. Vash queried non-existent authorities, but Steward Nine-Grudge refused to grant arch access without cause. When Ilm publicly voiced a grievance regarding the Line’s halt, the Steward agreed to escort them. The cohort followed the Steward through the Warden arch onto the Print Floor, leaving the feeders and silent machines behind.
