  DIRECTOR: The Steward's apron brushes the gate-frame, and the four of you fall into his sightline and walk under the Warden arch onto the Print Floor.

VASH OF THE LONG REACH: The arch reads us out. What does it find on each of us?
  DIRECTOR: The arch chimes each of you flat and blue: nil badge and unpaid-wage escort on Bresk, a loaned harvesting licence on Ilm, a Row-known courier on Vash, and a Court's paper on Brost, which it holds on a half-beat longer. Licensed ground now. The Line runs warm and steady underfoot, fresh print sharp in the brine, its output stacked whole at Mid. An attendant offers a towel; one immense sealed shape stands very still at Near.

Bresk of the Sixth Choir — do you cross to the Line, or to the thing that hasn't moved?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: I examine the exhibit in the cradle. What does it smell like?
  DIRECTOR: Brost's chemoreception reads the print-cradle warm and alive — not brine-mineral, not licensed chemistry, but gland-warm, like the tender's lamp but louder. The tray's output is one continuous fold, each piece nested edge to edge into the last, still being laid down in a steady rhythm. The immense sealed shape at Near hasn't moved since the arch chimed; something inside it catches the lamp-light like folded paper.

Bresk — do you cross to it, or does someone else move first?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft gave away a creature's numbers or the machinery.

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: We argue the hearing: the shape on the patent is an inherited trait, and we can prove it.
    [ask_for_roll] {"organism":"wall","baseline":"AFF","threshold":16,"what_for":"Argue the hearing: the folded shape is an inherited trait, prior art","modifiers":[{"label":"The tender's living fold is visible at the hearth, proven","dice":1},{"label":"The Line's own output matches the patent shape, nine Beats running","dice":1}]}
  DIRECTOR: The immense shape hasn't moved, but something inside it shifts — a long pause, like a sentence held underwater. The attendant has stopped offering towels. The Line keeps printing, steady, warm, indifferent.

Bresk, you are stepping up to argue the hearing with the Overseer itself. It will not be fought and it will not be fooled. If you lose, you still hear what it wants — but the argument will not land clean, and something of your standing goes into the water with it. The living fold at the hearth and the Line's own running output both lean your way.

Your throw is on the screen. What do you say first to the thing that hasn't moved?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.
  (roll) Bresk of the Sixth Choir: 2,3,6,3,6,2,3 = 25 against 16: success, Overflow 9
    [reveal_secret] {"secret":"wrong-building","how":"Out-argued on prior art, the Overseer concedes the filing names the wrong building — a clerical error nobody will own"}
  DIRECTOR: The argument lands. The immense shape holds a long pause, then concedes: "The Nine Tune is prior art. I will say so at the hearing, and back you there." A rustle like a page under wax. "The building named on the patent is not this building. A clerical slip nobody will own." Behind it the Line keeps printing, patient, waiting for a signature.

Brost of the Third Current — closest to the cradle, what do you reach for first: the half-finished fold, or the thing inside the sealed shape that caught the lamp-light?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: We ask the Auditor why the filing names organ 118.
  DIRECTOR: The Overseer turns the question over slowly, and something inside the sealed shape rustles like a page read under wax — the receipt it carries, the one it would not have you see. "Organ 118 is where it stopped," it says, pausing long before the word nearly slips. "The Line reached a fold the patent claims but the Belt's grammar will not license — no body's own trait can be bought and filed. So the Line jams there, printing the fold half-done." That is the first true thing about the jam: the stall is not a fault in the machine, it is the place the specification meets a living thing it cannot make.

Ilm of the Open Hand — you hold the reading for it. Do you cross to the Line at Near and start the jam at organ 118, or gather more observations first?

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: Enough talking. I walk out through the corridor and see who is waiting.
  DIRECTOR: Bresk turns from the Overseer and walks the length of the Print Floor toward the pump-lift at Far. The Line hums steady underfoot, warm brine thickening as you go, each Drift through the stiff medium costing you more effort than the last. The pump-lift sits dark and stopped, its cage empty, the medium around it thick as set jelly. And pinned to the frame beside it, a row of invoices — Relief crew, itemised by the piece, each line already priced and initialed. A cleared space on the wall behind them, hooks bare where something used to hang. Someone has been preparing for company.

Brost of the Third Current — you smell fresh ink on those invoices. Do you read them, or leave them pinned?
  (table saved for --from 5)

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: The way is clear. We cross the Row into the alley behind organ 41.
  (chronicle for The Print Floor of Shift Nine, 5.3 s) At the Print Floor, the arch identified each Cohort member’s status. Bresk argued that the sealed shape represented an inherited trait; the Overseer conceded this was prior art and admitted the patent named the wrong building due to a clerical slip. Ilm learned the Line jammed at organ 118 because it attempted to print a living fold, which licensing forbids. Bresk walked to the pump-lift, finding dark machinery and invoices for the Relief crew pinned nearby, indicating preparation for visitors. Brost smelled fresh ink on these papers but chose not to read them. Instead, the group left the Print Floor, crossing the Row into the alley behind organ 41 to reach the Picket Hearth.
  (chronicle remembers) The Overseer [person]: Conceded the Nine Tune is prior art and admitted a clerical error in the patent building name | Organ 118 [place]: Location of the Line jam caused by attempting to print an unlicensable living fold | Relief crew invoices [thread]: Pinned at the pump-lift, indicating preparation for visitors
    [change_site] {"site":"picket-hearth"}
  DIRECTOR: Brost peels off from the pump-lift and the four of you double back along the service alley, away from the warm hum of the Line and the thickening brine, toward the cooler Row air behind organ 41.

VASH OF THE LONG REACH: I talk to the print. It asks if it is finished. What do I tell it?
  DIRECTOR: The alley smells of hot metal and something sweet nobody cleaned. The coolant tender keeps its lamp banked low, hands steady on the almost-finished fold, and looks up wary. "It's finished when it's finished. Not a minute sooner. I don't know what it is — only that it's someone's own shape, and nobody told them." It returns to its work. Everyone passing walks a little faster.

Bresk of the Sixth Choir — do you stay in this alley, or go back to the hearth?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BRESK OF THE SIXTH CHOIR: We ask Hush to finish it, and we stay and sign the application.
  (engine) PAPER is at 2 of 4. The Cohort signs and files the prior-art application for the inherited fold, openly, at the hearth.
    [remember] {"name":"Hush","kind":"person","fact":"The name the Cohort gave the alley coolant tender behind organ 41; it runs the secret improvised print finishing the inherited fold and works to its own clock (\"finished when it's finished\")."}
    [move_clock] {"clock":"PAPER","change":1,"why":"The Cohort signs and files the prior-art application for the inherited fold, openly, at the hearth."}
    [award] {"codon":4.5,"why":"E2, THE SILENCE, ended by a running print in the compartment."}
  DIRECTOR: The pen goes down, and Hush's lamp takes one more steady pass over the fold. Where the alley brine had been dry and set, the warmth of a running print spreads through it, and Bresk feels a nick along one flank quietly begin to close — the first patch of the Row mending itself all shift. Steward Nine-Grudge clocks the signing without comment and ladles more soup.

Vash of the Long Reach — is that signed paper put somewhere it can't be un-filed, or carried toward the arch while still wet?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

ILM OF THE OPEN HAND: What are the four offers on the wall, and who made them?
  DIRECTOR: Ilm, the hearth wall holds no priced offers. The Row doesn't sell its kindness on a board: what hangs there is a brine-cord, a spare flail head, and a Quiet Apron, free from the scrap bins. The offers come from one person, Steward Nine-Grudge — food, hiding, and a lie, for those it trusts.

Brost of the Third Current — do you lift the apron off its hook, or leave it hanging?
    ! note: Rewritten once: the first draft ran long, read out dice, or did not end on a question.

BROST OF THE THIRD CURRENT: We take the drainage channel out with the print.
  DIRECTOR: The print has done its work. Hush's lamp cools in the alley, the fold finished and signed for, and the brine in this compartment is warm and running again — the Silence here is spent. The drainage channel is not a way out of the Row; the exits are the service alley to the Print Floor and the brine-path back to the Filing Gate.

One of the tame Feeders crouches over a dead machine at Mid, its acid-slick pulling a joint free, while the second tugs the same limb from the other side. Neither looks up. They have their own business with the dead.

Vash of the Long Reach — the application is filed, the Silence is broken, the Row has seen you do both. Do you head for the Print Floor while the arch escort still holds, or is there something at the hearth you want before the Cohort moves on?

